Name :
================
UDLD (Unidirectional Link Detection)

Why we did this
================
STP protects against loops and Loop Guard protects against missing BPDUs.

However, there is a situation where a physical link remains UP but traffic flows in only one direction.

This is called a Unidirectional Link Failure.

Example:

Switch A ---> Switch B  ✅

Switch B ---> Switch A  ❌

The interface appears UP on both switches, but communication works in only one direction.

This can cause:

- STP failures
- Layer 2 loops
- Broadcast storms
- Traffic blackholing
- Network instability

To solve this problem, Cisco introduced UDLD.

UDLD actively verifies bidirectional communication between neighboring devices.

What will happen
================
1. UDLD packets are exchanged between neighboring devices.
2. Each switch verifies that its neighbor can hear it.
3. If communication becomes one-way:
   - UDLD detects the failure.
   - Port is marked as unhealthy.
   - In Aggressive Mode, the port is shut down.
4. Network loops are prevented.
5. STP remains stable.

Example

Normal Operation
----------------

Switch A <------> Switch B

UDLD Messages:
--------------
Sent and Received

Result:
--------
Link Healthy


Unidirectional Failure
----------------------

Switch A ------> Switch B

Switch A <------X Switch B

Result:
--------
UDLD detects failure

Port disabled

Loop prevented

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================

Advantages
----------
1. Detects one-way communication failures.
2. Prevents Layer 2 loops.
3. Improves network stability.
4. Protects fiber connections.
5. Works independently of STP.
6. Fast failure detection.
7. Recommended on trunk and fiber links.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. Must be enabled on both sides.
2. Additional protocol overhead.
3. Can disable links if misconfigured.
4. Not required on all access ports.
5. Requires support on neighboring devices.

Hence what came next
--------------------
UDLD protects physical links.

However, administrators also wanted to protect the network against excessive broadcast traffic generated by loops, malware, or misconfigured devices.

This led to:

Storm Control

which limits:
- Broadcast Traffic
- Multicast Traffic
- Unknown Unicast Traffic

and prevents network congestion.

One-Shot Configuration
======================

Enable UDLD Globally

Switch(config)# udld enable


Enable UDLD on Interface

Switch(config)# interface g0/1
Switch(config-if)# udld port


Enable Aggressive Mode (Recommended)

Switch(config)# interface g0/1
Switch(config-if)# udld port aggressive

Verification
============

show udld

show udld interface g0/1

show udld neighbors

